You can install Qdrant locally with Docker in just a few commands. Here’s the clean, correct setup.


1. Pull the Qdrant Docker image
docker pull qdrant/qdrant


2. Run Qdrant locally

The simplest way:

docker run -p 6333:6333 -p 6334:6334 qdrant/qdrant

This starts Qdrant with:

REST API on port 6333

gRPC API on port 6334

After starting, you can visit:
👉 http://localhost:6333/dashboard

to access the Qdrant web UI.

(Recommended) Run Qdrant with persistent storage

Otherwise, your data disappears when the container stops.

docker run -p 6333:6333 -p 6334:6334 \
  -v $(pwd)/qdrant_storage:/qdrant/storage \
  qdrant/qdrant


This maps a local folder ./qdrant_storage to Qdrant’s internal storage path.

4. Test your Qdrant instance
curl http://localhost:6333/collections


You should get a JSON response like:

{"collections": []}

. Use Qdrant from Python

Install the client:

pip install qdrant-client


Connect:

from qdrant_client import QdrantClient

client = QdrantClient("http://localhost:6333")


Create a collection example:

client.recreate_collection(
    collection_name="my_vectors",
    vectors_config={
        "size": 1536,   # embedding dimension
        "distance": "Cosine"
    }
)


Insert vectors:

client.upsert(
    collection_name="my_vectors",
    points=[
        {
            "id": 1,
            "vector": [0.1, 0.2, ...],
            "payload": {"text": "hello world"}
        }
    ]
)

🚀 You’re done — Qdrant is now running locally

